  "pages": 16,
  "question": "Why do the fractions 1/(2^n−1) over an infinite set of exponents n add up to an irrational number whenever 1/n, added up over the same exponents, is finite?",
  "precis": "Erdős asked whether infinitely many of the fractions 1, 1/3, 1/7, 1/15, …, each of the form 1/(2^n−1) and each used at most once, can ever add up to a fraction. The paper proves that the chosen fractions add up to an irrational number whenever 1/n, added up over the chosen exponents n, is finite; for the exponents 2, 4, 8, 16, … this makes 1/3+1/15+1/255+1/65535+… irrational. Sections 1 to 8 explain the proof with examples; Appendix A supplies supporting details and formal-proof correspondence. The finite reconstruction here is an ordinary proof; the linked Lean and Comparator receipts establish the formal theorem and its own proof. For an arbitrary infinite choice the question is still open.",
  "owns": "an account of Theorem A.1 of the #257 short paper at base 2 for a reader new to the problem: a guided proof through worked examples, with supporting details, exercises and the formal-proof correspondence in appendices",
  "not_authority_for": "the validity of the Lean theorem it quotes, which belongs to the cited kernel-checked source, or a solution to Erdős #257, which remains open",
